<TopicPage slug="subjects/cognitive-science/foundational/what-is-cognitive-science">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Cognitive science is built on one wager: that minds are systems which process information, and
    can therefore be studied the way other complex systems are — with models, measurements and
    experiments. Psychology, neuroscience, linguistics, philosophy and computer science each hold
    one end of the problem; the field is the triangulation between them.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the founding wager, unpacked" widget="equationStep" config={cogsciCfg} />

  <h2>Levels: why three true descriptions don't conflict</h2>
  <p>
    Why did you remember your password? Psychologically: it is in long-term memory, cued by the
    login screen. Biologically: synapses in your hippocampus strengthened through consolidation.
    Computationally: a retrieval algorithm matched a key. None of these is "the real one" —
    cognitive science's great lesson is that explanations live at levels, and confusing them is the
    field's classic error.
  </p>

  <h2>The classic questions on the table</h2>
  <p>
    The core curriculum circles a fixed set of puzzles: How does perception build a world from
    photons? Why is attention so narrow? What are memory's systems and why do they fail? How do
    children acquire language from noisy input? How do people actually reason — and why do the
    heuristics that usually work also produce systematic biases?
  </p>

  <h2>Models that run</h2>
  <p>
    What distinguishes the field from armchair philosophy is executable theory. Symbolic models
    captured reasoning as rules; connectionist networks showed learning without programming;
    Bayesian models formalised the mind as an inference engine; and modern large language models
    have become unexpected psychological subjects — systems that behave, in ways, like language
    users. Every model is a hypothesis precise enough to fail.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> MIT Brain &amp; Cognitive Sciences course sequence (OCW); Thagard, <em>Mind: Introduction to Cognitive Science</em>; Miller, "The Cognitive Revolution" (2003).</p>
</TopicPage>
